\\\r\\\\nBuilding Code of Australia 1996 Volume Two \\\\r\\\\nFigure \\\\r\\\\nSPACING OF ACCESSIBLE HORIZONTAL MEMBERS, PROJECTIONS OR \\\\r\\\\nINDENTATIONS IN FENCING \\\\r\\\\nFigure \\\\r\\\\nCHAIN WIRE OR MESH FENCE \\\\r\\\\nFencing material with an aperture more than 13 mm, but ess than 100 mm\\\\r\\\\nClause 3.9.3.2 \\\\r\\\\nSUPERSEDED SUPERSEDED\\\",\\\"section\\\":null,\\\"score\\\":1,\\\"originalUrl\\\":\\\"/api/standards-library?document=768aecc777a252ae8aea7debaebdb0c194faf7dab94420b164422ec4d412d823#page=350\\\"}],\\\"scope\\\":\\\"Source excerpts only. Verify printed title, edition, jurisdiction and applicability before relying on a reference.\\\"}\"},{\"kind\":\"assistant\",\"tool\":null,\"failed\":null,\"origin\":null,\"projectName\":null,\"projectRevision\":null,\"text\":\"The local NCC library only carries building-code excerpts about pool/barrier fencing apertures; it does not hold the chain-wire product standard itself. I do not have a live web browser in this session, and I will not invent a designation, year or URL.\\n\\nHere is what I can confirm from the evidence I actually retrieved.\\n\\n## What the local standards library returned\\n\\nThe only on-device references that match \\\"chain wire mesh aperture\\\" are Building Code of Australia (BCA / NCC) Volume Two clauses on pool-safety barrier fencing — not the product standard for chain-wire mesh. Every excerpt carries the same Figure caption:\\n\\n- Source (retrieved now): on-device BCA/NCC PDF, search_standards_library topic \\\"chain wire fencing mesh aperture\\\".\\n- The note reads: \\\"Fencing material with an aperture more than 13 mm, but less than 100 mm\\\" (Figure 3.9.3.3, \\\"Chain wire or mesh fence\\\").\\n\\nRetrieved page URLs (all carry the watermark \\\"SUPERSEDED SUPERSEDED\\\" and are BCA 1996 amendments or BCA 2004–2009 Volume Two):\\n\\n- BCA 1996 Volume Two Amendment 0, p. 340 — sha256 a5574327…b6c4e\\n- BCA 1996 Volume Two Amendment 1, p. 350 — sha256 768aecc7…d823